5.Joins & Triggers:
So, we can use Join and Trigger to perform ETL, but both are used for the different purposes. Let take a look on both of the techniques one by one.



Joins:
In the Transform phase of the ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) process, a join is used to combine data from two or more tables into one unified result. This is done by matching columns that have related or common values. Essentially, joins help merge different pieces of information based on how they are logically connected, making it easier to work with and analyze the data. 

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Triggers:
A trigger is like an automatic system that performs certain actions or starts an ETL process when specific conditions or events happen. In databases, triggers are often set up to run certain tasks, like stored procedures or functions, whenever something important occurs, such as changes in the data. In the context of ETL, triggers can be used to initiate processes like data extraction or transformation as soon as certain conditions are met, making the process more efficient and timelier.

Note: This method or technique is slower than other method that we have to preform ETL.

Identifying the different loads:


Initial Load:
The initial load is the process of transferring all the data from one system to another for the first time. It's like moving everything from your old house into a new one when you’re just getting started. For example, if you're setting up a new data warehouse for an e-commerce site, the initial load would involve moving all the sales, customer, and product data from the old system into the new warehouse. This can take a lot of time and resources because you’re moving everything all at once. It’s often done when starting fresh with a new system or when switching from one system to another.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Incremental Load:
An incremental load is the process of updating your system by only adding or changing the data that has been updated or created since the last time you did the transfer. Instead of moving all the data every time, you only move the new or modified pieces, which makes it faster and more efficient.

For example, after the initial load of all your e-commerce data, an incremental load would only bring in the new sales orders or any updates to customer information that have occurred since the last update. This keeps your system up to date without having to reload everything. It’s typically done on a regular schedule, like daily or weekly. The challenge, though, is figuring out which data has changed, and that usually involves tracking methods like timestamps or specialized tools.
